{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And the officers shall add to speak to the people and they shall say, Who is the man that is afraid</b>: According to the opinion of Rabbi Yosi the Galilean (Sotah 44a), since the priest has promised that HaShem will deliver them and not a single man will be lost from among them, it is fitting that the righteous should trust in Him, and the officers warn the fearful against transgressions that are in their hands. Therefore the Torah gives him cause to return to his house, his vineyard, and his wife, in order to cover for the others who return on account of transgressions. And the reason for <b>lest he die</b> is that he may think thus in his heart and flee. And according to the opinion of Rabbi Akiva, it is as it sounds, for one who is afraid after the priest's promise does not trust in HaShem as is fitting, and no miracle will be performed for him." "And the reason for <b>and tender-hearted</b> is that it is not in his nature to see the blow of a sword and killing, for <b>the fearful</b> is one who does not trust, and he sends him back because of his insufficient trust, while <b>the tender-hearted</b> is because of the weakness of his nature, for he may flee or faint. And they said in the Jerusalem Talmud (Sotah 8:9) that all of them must bring proof for their words — that is, they must bring witnesses concerning the house, the vineyard, and the wife before the commander of the army, and then he grants them permission to return. And so they said also according to the opinion of Rabbi Yosi the Galilean, that one must bring proof concerning the transgression that is in his hand, for otherwise the majority of the people would return with false claims. But according to the words of Rabbi Akiva, regarding faintness of heart such that one cannot stand in the ranks of battle and see a drawn sword, he said that one need not bring proof, for his witnesses are with him." "<b>And let him not melt the heart of his brethren</b>: The author of the Halakhot Gedolot made this a negative commandment — that one may not refrain from returning, lest he melt the heart of his brethren like his own heart. And this is like <b>and it shall not be eaten, its flesh</b> (Exodus 21:28)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:52:20.972669498Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}